      "title": "Read the whole contract",
      "narration": "Aisha and Marcus find a policy phrase they understand differently. Disagreement alone does not make language ambiguous. Read the words in their ordinary meaning and in the context of the whole policy. A genuine ambiguity can exist when language reasonably supports more than one meaning. Interpretation rules can protect reasonable expectations and resolve ambiguity, but do not assume a customer's hope overrides a clear exclusion. In Maryland, courts do not simply discard clear policy wording because one party expected broader protection.",
