      "title": "How Maryland resolves an ambiguity",
      "text": "Maryland first reads the contract as a whole using ordinary meaning. A genuine ambiguity can permit extrinsic evidence of the parties’ intent. If ambiguity remains after that evidence is considered, it ordinarily is resolved against the drafter; the court does not begin by automatically reading every clause against the insurer.",
      "example": "A clearly stated benefit limit does not become ambiguous merely because a customer hoped for a larger payment.",
